            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>What to Do First When Your Account Is Suspended</h2>
<p>Don't rush to write an appeal email—first, <strong>confirm the violation type</strong>. Go to your Google Ads backend and find the "Policy Decisions" page. Google will clearly state the violation reason: circumvention, suspicious content, trademark infringement, or landing page issues. Different violation types require different appeal channels and materials. Submitting to the wrong channel will only result in a faster rejection.</p>
<h2>What Are the Most Critical Documents for an Appeal</h2>
<p><strong>Appeal form + corrective evidence</strong>—both are essential. The appeal form is the entry point, but corrective evidence is what convinces the reviewer. Choose evidence based on violation type: for landing page issues, provide screenshots of modified pages and URLs; for content violations, attach new creatives with explanatory notes; for trademark or copyright complaints, provide authorization documents or withdrawal agreements. All materials must be clearly readable, and screenshots should include timestamps.</p>
<h2>What Are the Taboos in Writing an Appeal Email</h2>
<p>Don't write lengthy apology letters. Reviewers handle hundreds of cases daily—no one has time to read your emotional journey. Avoid statements like "the system made a mistake" or "competitors filed malicious reports." The correct approach: <strong>briefly state the violation reason (using Google's official terminology), clearly state the corrective actions taken, and attach evidence links or documents</strong>. Keep the email under 200 words, structure it clearly, and lead with the conclusion.</p>
<h2>Can You Continue Appealing After Multiple Rejections</h2>
<p>Yes, but with conditions. For second and subsequent appeals, you must <strong>address the specific reasons for the previous rejection</strong> rather than resubmitting unchanged materials. Common supplementary materials include: third-party compliance certifications, industry qualification certificates, third-party platform store links, or user review screenshots. If rejected three times, pause and investigate deeper issues (such as website server IP or payment page interface flags) before continuing—repeated submissions may mark you as "abusing the appeal channel."</p>
<h2>Can You Request a Refund of Account Balance During the Appeal</h2>
<p>Yes. Account suspension doesn't void your balance. During or after the appeal, you can request a <strong>balance refund</strong> from Google support, typically requiring your account ID, top-up records, and a refund reason. Refund cycles are generally 15-30 business days, returned to the original payment method. If your balance is significant, consider opening a new account in parallel to avoid ad interruptions during the refund period.</p>
<h2>How Do Material Requirements Differ by Violation Type</h2>
<p><strong>Content violations</strong> require corrected creatives and explanatory documents; <strong>trademark/copyright complaints</strong> need authorization documents or withdrawal agreements; <strong>circumvention</strong> is more complex and requires domain registration info, server hosting proof, ICP filing or foreign compliance documents, plus explanations of whether the violation was due to technical loopholes or third-party platform issues. If your account has multiple violation types simultaneously, submit separate appeals—one email per issue—to avoid material confusion that extends review cycles.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Account appeals aren't about luck—they're about precision in material preparation. Understanding the violation reason in advance, preparing targeted evidence, and keeping emails concise are the three keys to improving approval rates.</p>
